     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">OK, we&#39;ll give it a try! Thanks for all the help</div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Fri, Feb 16, 2018 at 1:51 PM, Erik Westlund <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">edbwe...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr\">\r\n<span><span></span></span>\r\n<div>\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr\">Yes. You can point different requests to different servers. Here you’d put a rewrite rule to /PSESV1 that proxies requests to shiny. This is ideal because you then don’t need to worry about cross-site weirdness with requests between\r\n them.</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr\">Erik</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Get <a href=\"https://aka.ms/o0ukef\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://aka.ms/o0ukef&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792388165942000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0GgP_vi0W_7vTu4lpajNNN\">Outlook for iOS</a></div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<hr style=\"display:inline-block;width:98%\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><font face=\"Calibri, sans-serif\" color=\"#000000\" style=\"font-size:11pt\"><b>From:</b> Jean Lee - NOAA Affiliate &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jean...@noaa.gov</a>&gt;<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> Friday, February 16, 2018 3:02:28 PM<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> Erik Westlund<br>\r\n<b>Cc:</b> Shiny - Web Framework for R<br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> Re: publishing PHP pages on Shiny server?</font>\r\n<div> </div>\r\n</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div><div><div>\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">Yes, we have access to the shiny server (currently installed on LInux OS) and have technical resources available to install nginx/Apache and configure mySQL user permissions.</div>\r\n<div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\"><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">Any issues with using iframes and bootstrap together?</div>\r\n<div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\"><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\">Would it be possible with proxying to have journal pages and shiny visualizations residing on a separate servers, but both accessed from the same root URL? E.g.,\r\n<a href=\"http://psesv.psfmc.org/index.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://psesv.psfmc.org/index.php&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792388165942000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0TR29GqxBiCP5Rx1CHFHB4\">psesv.psfmc.org/index.php</a> is the journal home page URL, and\r\n<a href=\"http://psesv.psmfc.org/PSESV1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://psesv.psmfc.org/PSESV1&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792388165942000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3RIruRrq5aBts_RpbfI80U\">psesv.psmfc.org/PSESV1</a> is the page for a shiny visualization.</div>\r\n<div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\"><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:arial,helvetica,sans-serif\"><br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
